    "sec.comp.h": "Compare points", "sec.comp.p": "Each line shows the likely range of change in heavy-rain days per decade. If the line touches 0, we cannot be sure there was a change.",
    "cmp.cap": "Summary of all points with data", "cmp.none": "Once there are points with data you can compare them here.", "cmp.point": "Point", "cmp.prov": "Province", "cmp.ext": "Heavy days/year", "cmp.change": "Change/decade", "cmp.ci": "95% range", "cmp.p": "Adjusted p", "cmp.state": "Status",
    "apr.kicker": "Starting from zero", "apr.h": "Learn how it works", "apr.p": "You don't need to know about climate or statistics. Here we explain, in plain words, where the data comes from and how to read it.",
    "apr.intro.t": "What is Phyllobytes?", "apr.intro.p": "It is an atlas that shows how it has rained in Costa Rica. It uses data measured by NASA satellites and turns it into maps and charts. Its question is simple: are there more, fewer or the same number of heavy-rain days as before?",
    "apr.flow.h": "From satellite to your screen", "apr.flow.sub": "This is how the information travels, in five steps.",
    "apr.flow.1.t": "Satellites watch the rain", "apr.flow.1.d": "NASA's GPM mission and other satellites estimate from space how much rain falls. From them the IMERG product is built.",
    "apr.flow.2.t": "NASA POWER delivers it by coordinate", "apr.flow.2.d": "This free NASA service gives the rain of each day for any point. Each point represents a cell of about 10 km.",
    "apr.flow.3.t": "Phyllobytes downloads and tidies it", "apr.flow.3.d": "A small program on your computer (the Python server) asks for the data, checks that no days are missing and saves copies as TXT and CSV files.",
    "apr.flow.4.t": "Heavy-rain days are counted", "apr.flow.4.d": "For each place we define what “heavy rain” is (the most intense 5% of rainy days from 2001 to 2020) and count how many such days there were each year.",
    "apr.flow.5.t": "We work out if it rises, falls or stays", "apr.flow.5.d": "A statistical test checks whether the count shows a real trend or just normal variation. Then everything is drawn on the map and charts.",
    "apr.use.h": "How to use the map", "apr.use.sub": "Four steps to your first result.",
    "apr.use.1": "Tap anywhere in Costa Rica on the map to mark a point. You can have up to 8.", "apr.use.2": "Press “Analyse with NASA data” to bring in the real rainfall of your points.",
    "apr.use.3": "Under “What to show on the heat map” pick an indicator and watch the country get coloured.", "apr.use.4": "Tap a point to see its charts. You can copy its sheet or download its data.",
    "apr.heat.h": "How to read the heat map", "apr.heat.p1": "Colours go from dark (low values) to light (high values). The lines separate levels, just like the contour lines of a mountain. For change per decade, one colour means “falls”, another “rises” and the light centre “no change”.",
    "apr.heat.p2": "The colour between two points is a smoothed estimate: the more points you add, the more faithful it is. It never replaces a local measurement.",
    "apr.gloss.h": "Plain words",
    "apr.g.extremo.t": "Heavy-rain day", "apr.g.extremo.d": "A day with more rain than 95% of that place's rainy days. It is “heavy” for that spot, not for the whole country.",
    "apr.g.percentil.t": "95th percentile", "apr.g.percentil.d": "The limit that separates the most intense 5% of rainy days from the rest.",
    "apr.g.tendencia.t": "Trend", "apr.g.tendencia.d": "The direction a number moves over the years: up, down or steady.",
    "apr.g.intervalo.t": "Likely range", "apr.g.intervalo.d": "The spread of reasonable values for the change. If it includes 0, there may be no real change.",
    "apr.g.pvalor.t": "P-value", "apr.g.pvalor.d": "How easily we could see that trend just by chance. Below 0.05 is taken as evidence.",
    "apr.g.celda.t": "10 km cell", "apr.g.celda.d": "Satellite data averages a square about 10 km on a side; it does not measure a street or a farm.",
    "apr.g.calor.t": "Heat map", "apr.g.calor.d": "A map that colours the territory by a value, estimating between the points you have.",
    "apr.g.demo.t": "Demo data", "apr.g.demo.d": "Made-up numbers to show how the page looks without a server. It is not real rain.",
    "apr.eco": "Caring for water starts with understanding it: knowing how rainfall changes helps communities, rivers and forests prepare. Phyllobytes is an educational tool; it does not predict rain or replace official institutions.",
    "apr.cta.t": "Any questions left? Ask the assistant.", "apr.cta.btn": "Open the assistant",
    "sec.met.h": "Method and limits", "sec.met.p": "Four steps anyone can repeat, and what this atlas cannot conclude.", "met.si": "What it shows", "met.no": "What it does not show",
    "paso1.t": "Local threshold", "paso1.d": "For each point we take the 95th percentile of rainy days (1 mm or more) in the baseline period, up to 20 years from the start. That value stays fixed.",
    "paso2.t": "Yearly count", "paso2.d": "Days above the threshold are counted year by year. Incomplete years are excluded, not filled in.",
    "paso3.t": "Trend", "paso3.d": "The Theil–Sen slope estimates days per decade and the Mann–Kendall test checks whether it can be told apart from chance.",
    "paso4.t": "Several points", "paso4.d": "Because several points are tested at once, p-values are adjusted with the Holm method.",
    "si1": "Observed changes in the heavy-rain days of each point.", "si2": "How much it changed, with its likely range and adjusted p-value.", "si3": "A comparison between places to decide where it is worth studying more.",
    "no1": "It is not a forecast or a flood alert.", "no2": "It does not describe a neighbourhood or a farm: each point is a ~10 km satellite cell.", "no3": "It does not identify causes or prove climate change.", "no4": "An inconclusive trend does not prove there was no change.",
    "src.product": "Source", "src.demo": "DEMO (made-up data)", "src.res": "Detail", "src.resv": "~0.1° (~10 km)", "src.period": "Period", "src.unit": "Unit", "src.generated": "Generated", "src.demo_gen": "When the page opened", "src.note": "Note",
